Nutrition in the sidebar has three tabs.

## Meal plans

Your meal plans, each with a line saying what it is ("Training day 2,100 kcal · rest day 1,850 ·
5 meals · Adaptive targets") and how many clients have it. A meal plan can be just targets
(calories and protein, say) or full meals: the same thing, more filled in. If you don't coach food,
you never need this page; clients log against their own targets.

A plan's targets: calories you set, or **Adaptive targets** that follow each client's own goal pace;
protein, carbs and fat (leave one empty and they keep their own); and more targets, at least, at
most or about: fibre, salt, sugar, saturated fat, and vitamins and minerals. Many foods don't list
vitamins, so a vitamin target is only as good as the labels. Tick what the plan is good for
(dairy-free, vegetarian…). Changes save as you go.

## Building a meal plan

Open a meal plan to build it. First, how days work:

- **Same every day**: one set of meals and targets.
- **Training and rest days**: two, by whether the day has training (their program's weekdays, or
  any day they train). Switching copies today's meals into both, ready to change.
- **Your own day types**: high carb, low carb, refeed… each on the weekdays you pick. A weekday has
  one type, so picking it for a new one takes it off the other.

Each day has its targets (calories, and protein, carbs and fat; empty means their own) and its
meals. A meal has a time: a clock time, so many minutes before training, within so long after, or
none. "Training" is their session time, the start time they set for each program day on their
phone, so if their session moves, the meal moves with it; on a rest day, before/after meals use the
plan's rest-day time (16:30 unless you change it).

**+ Food or recipe** searches the same foods as the diary and your recipes (filtered by the plan's
"good for" tags). Set the amount (grams, or servings for a recipe) and add swaps clients can have
instead. A food's ⋯ adds a swap, marks it as not fitting a tag ("not dairy-free"), or moves it.
The day total shows the meals against the targets, and warns when a food doesn't fit the plan's
tags; clients who said they don't eat it only see its swap. **Grocery list for a week** adds up
each day type's meals for its days, with recipes as their ingredients.

## Recipes

Your recipes are the same ones as in your own diary. Each has its ingredients for the whole recipe,
how many servings it makes, how long it takes, a method, a photo, and what it's good for; one
serving is worked out from the ingredients. The list shows where each is used in your meal plans.
Adding a recipe to a meal plan copies it, so changing the recipe later doesn't change plans
you've given.

## Rules

How targets are set: the built-in Adaptive targets (a standard formula first, then corrected from
their weight and food trend after about two weeks), with your trend window for new plans, and any
nutrition plugins you've added. Food swaps: only your swaps, or your swaps then foods close to the
plan (the same kind of food, within 15% of the calories and protein). Logging anything else is
always allowed; it shows as off plan.

## Giving a meal plan

**Give to a client…** (on a meal plan, its ⋯ in the list, or a client's Nutrition tab) gives each
client their own copy; you can change theirs without touching yours. For each client it shows:

- what the plan works out to for them: each day type's calories (Adaptive targets from their
  weight and diary, when they have a goal) and protein, and which days are training days;
- a check against their start-up answers: food they said they don't eat that's in the plan, and
  whether they'll only see its swap;
- a warning when a day comes out below their resting burn (worked out from their own goal's
  height, age and weight). You can still give it.

If they already have a plan, choose when the new one starts: from tomorrow, from today, or from the
day the old one started, when it was the wrong plan. Then their past days are measured against the
new targets instead, in their charts, your Nutrition tab and Adaptive targets; the food they logged
and their weight entries never change. You can add a message. They get one notification.

When you change a plan clients have copies of, **Update N clients…** brings their copies up to
your version from their next day. A copy you changed for one client is left as it is unless you
tick it.

## A client's Nutrition tab

Their plan (their copy, since when, what it works out to), with **Edit their copy** (the same
builder, for them only) and **Change plan**. If they share their food diary: the last 7 days, each
against the target that applied that day (training or rest, and the plan they had then), how many
of the plan's meals they logged, and a comment on any day, which goes to your messages and shows on
that day in their app. Then their weight trend against their goal pace.

Goals are theirs; targets and programs are yours. **Suggest a goal change…** sends them your
suggestion (a pace, a target weight, why); they use it or keep theirs, and you're told either way.
Adaptive targets aim at their goal pace, so if the pace looks wrong, suggest a change rather than
fight it with targets.
